</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robust Statistical Testing.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A naive way to detect the watermark would rely on counting occurrences of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="red" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">red</annotation></semantics></math> list tokens (line 11). However, this approach might not be reliable due to the inherent problem that tokens generated by an IAR during image generation might not entirely match the tokens resulting from encoding the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exact same</span> generated image afterwards. This effect is caused by imperfections in the encoding and decoding from continuous image tokens to discrete token ids. We quantify this effect in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Ablations for Watermarking in IARs ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and find that the inherent token mismatch is substantial, making watermark detection in IARs inherently more challenging than for LLMs where each token id has an exact match with a discrete textual token—making encoding the same sequence <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lossless</span> in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, we find that we can overcome the problem in IARs by relying on robust statistical testing, following the approach by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, used in LLMs to prevent paraphrasing or synonym replacement attacks.
Therefore, we make our
<a href="#algorithm2" title="In Detecting the Watermark. ‣ 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Watermarking Method for IARs ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> return the output from the statistical test instead.
The null hypothesis is that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}:</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The sequence of tokens is generated with no knowledge about the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="red" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">red</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> lists</span>. Since the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="red" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">red</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> lists are selected at random, a non-watermarked image is expected to consists of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma T</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\gamma)T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\gamma)T</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="red" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">red</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\sum_{i=1}^{K}t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\sum_{i=1}^{K}t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of tokens for the image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="im" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">im</annotation></semantics></math>. The watermarked image should consists of a significantly more <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> tokens that a non-watermarked image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More formally, the color (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="red" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">red</annotation></semantics></math>) of the next token is a random variable <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that follows the Bernoulli distribution. For the non-watermarked image, the expectation (mean) is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[X]=\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[X]=\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and the Variance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Var[X]=\gamma(1-\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Var[X]=\gamma(1-\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. The color for all the tokens of image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="im" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">im</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined as another random variable <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> that follows the Binomial distribution with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[Y]=\gamma T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[Y]=\gamma T</annotation></semantics></math> and the Variance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Var[X]=\gamma(1-\gamma)T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Var[X]=\gamma(1-\gamma)T</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that a non-watermarked image would consists of only the tokens from the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="green" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">green</annotation></semantics></math> list is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is extremely small. For example, for an image with the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, we obtain <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=680" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>680</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=680</annotation></semantics></math> tokens and this probability <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> would be as low as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{680}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>680</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{680}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This enables us to detect the watermark by rejecting the null hypothesis <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and compute the z-statistics:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(|s|_{G}-\gamma T)/\sqrt{T\gamma(1-\gamma)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(|s|_{G}-\gamma T)/\sqrt{T\gamma(1-\gamma)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of the green tokens in the encoded image. We detect the watermark when the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is rejected for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is some pre-defined threshold.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Radioactive Watermarks</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having proposed WIAR, the first watermarking method for IARs, we are equipped with watermarking techniques for all the SOTA image generative models.
We now formally define the problem of watermark radioactivity in image generative models, discuss its motivation and relevance, and characterize the threat model considered in this work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Statement.</span>
Consider an image generative model <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, trained on a dataset <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (please see <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), that has been <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">watermarked</span> using a method <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. This model is then used to generate a collection of images <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which inherently carry the watermark, <em id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, an appropriate detection algorithm, specific to <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, can successfully identify the watermark signal in <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is used as a training dataset for a second generative model, <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to determine whether the watermark signal persists in the images <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the watermark remains detectable in <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the watermarking method <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to be <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">radioactive</span>, indicating that the watermark transfers through generated data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation and Relevance.</span> Radioactivity in image generative models is essential for ensuring that watermarking remains effective even when generated images are used as training data for new models. Without it, watermarking can only detect direct misuse, such as unauthorized image distribution, but fails when images are integrated into downstream training pipelines—leaving them vulnerable to untraceable and unauthorized reuse. By enabling persistent watermark traces, radioactivity helps creators, dataset curators, and model developers protecting their intellectual property and enables provenance tracking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threat Model.</span>
We consider a realistic threat model in which the intermediate images <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by model <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are neither retained nor available for verifying watermarks in <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Model <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may be accessible via a public API, without any assumptions about user accounts or associated metadata, or deployed locally on-premise. Additionally, we assume only black-box access to model <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning we can generate images but have no knowledge of its architecture, logits, or other internal states.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Empirical Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present our setup, main results, and ablations regarding radioactivity and performance of our WIAR watermark.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and Datasets.</span>
We evaluate the transferability of watermarks across DMs and IARs. Our study includes two types of DMs: the one operating in image (pixel) space and the other in latent space. For the former, we employ an EDM model architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and the CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. For the latter, we follow the setup from the previous work on watermarking for LDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and use Stable Diffusion 2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> with the MS COCO 2014 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> dataset.
For IARs, we consider VAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and RAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>
as two representative architectures, using the code and models provided in their respective code repositories. We run the IAR experiments using ImageNet.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermarking Methods.</span>
We investigate five watermarking methods, including four watermarking approaches for DMs, namely, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recipe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stable Signature</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PRC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tree-Ring</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, as well as WIAR, our new watermark for IARs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Radioactivity Evaluation.</span> For the <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recipe</span> framework on EDMs, as proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, the first model <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained unconditionally on the watermarked images <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In accordance with the method, we embed the watermark directly into the training data <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then generate 50000 images, matching the number of training samples used for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, to train a second model, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the same hyperparameters.
For <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recipe</span> on LDMs, we proceed similarly, however, due to the higher image resolution (32x32 in EDMs vs. 512x512 in LDMs), training from scratch is too computationally expensive. To simulate training from scratch we full fine-tune the models. In the case of <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stable Signature</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PRC</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tree-Ring</span>, the watermark is directly embedded into <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s outputs. Consequently, we only fine-tune <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the MS COCO captions. We follow a similar procedure for IARs. The outputs from <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are watermarked and then used to train the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> model. In the case of LDMs and IARs, we also examine an extreme scenario where DM <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the case of <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recipe</span>) is fine-tuned on a single image for an exceptionally high (800) number of epochs to <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">enforce</span> the transfer of a specific watermark instance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We report the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPR@FPR=1%</span>, which measures the true positive rate (TPR) at a 1% false positive rate (FPR) when detecting watermarked images. In this case, TPR = 1% corresponds to random guessing, whereas a significantly higher TPR (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&gt;&gt;1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;&gt;1\%</annotation></semantics></math>) indicates a robust watermark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the detectability of the watermarks, we conduct a statistical test using a <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">z-test</span> (see <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Robust Statistical Testing. ‣ 3.2 Watermark Detection ‣ 3 Watermarking Method for IARs ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The null hypothesis <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> states: <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”The watermark is not present in the model outputs if the TPR<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>1%@FPR=1%.”</span>
Similarly to the statistical test in WIAR, we set <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=4</annotation></semantics></math> (following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>), meaning that z-values below this threshold indicate a clear detection of the watermark in the outputs from <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or strong radioactivity in the outputs from <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The threshold ensures the low probability <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> of detecting the watermarks or radioactivity (incorrectly rejecting <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) when none is present in the image. Conversely, when the z-value is above the threshold <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
then the test is inconclusive and most likely the watermark was not present in the image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Analyzing Watermark Radioactivity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the watermark radioactivity in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.2 Analyzing Watermark Radioactivity ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The results show that the existing watermarks for EDMs, as well as our new watermark for IARs transfer. In contrast, watermarking schemes for LDMs are not radioactive.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Watermarks are Radioactive for EDMs and IARs but not for LDMs.<span id="S5.T1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We analyze five different watermarking methods for three different types of image generative models. We report TPR@FPR=1% for all methods (<em id="S5.T1.7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.T1.7.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, 1% corresponds to the random guessing baseline).</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Type of <math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Type of <math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
