<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Image Autoregressive Models (IARs)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RARs (Randomized Autoregressive Models) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> randomly order the tokens and then train the model to correctly predict the next token in the sequence. Each token is assigned a position in the sequence, so the pretext task in RARs facilitates the multidirectional representation since each token can be predicted from any set of previously given other tokens. This reflects the nature of images where a given part of the image might be influenced by any not necessarily neighboring parts.
The randomization of token order in RARs is slowly reduced (annealed) from fully random order to perfect raster-scan order.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Watermarking Properties</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In text-to-image models, watermarks are designed to be imperceptible to the human eye yet detectable by a specialized detection algorithms. Image watermarks must possess the following essential properties: (1) <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Undetectability</span> ensures that the watermark remains imperceptible to unauthorized parties, (2) <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unforgeability</span> guarantees that an adversary cannot reproduce the same watermark, (3)
<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tamper-evidence</span> allows for the detection of any modifications to the watermark, and (4) <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Robustness</span> ensures that the watermark remains detectable even under adversarial attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Analysis of Radioactivity for DIAGNOSIS</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The DIAGNOSIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> method detects unauthorized data usage by injecting memorization into text-to-image DMs when they are trained on protected images. For this goal, the protected images are coated using the WaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> injection function. A binary classifier is then trained on the coated and original images. When a DM <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is fine-tuned on the protected images, then the generated images, will be classifiable by the trained binary classifier to detect if the model has been trained with protected images.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze if this method is radioactive, we used the code from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> to train Stable Diffusion v1.4 for 100 epochs on 783 coated images from the CelebA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> dataset with unconditional triggering, a learning rate of 0.0001, a mini-batch of size 1 and using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> fine-tuning.
After training the first DM <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate 783 images, with the prompts of the coated images. On these generated images, we evaluate the memorization strength using the binary classifier, which reliably classifies the model as malicious with a <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> memorization strength. An example image can be seen in <a href="#A2.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ Appendix B Analysis of Radioactivity for DIAGNOSIS ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>. Then we train a second DM <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the images generated by DM <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the same hyperparameters, to analyze the transfer capabilities of the DIAGNOSIS method. Using the binary classifier on the images generated by DM <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, results in a memorization strength of <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the threshold by which a model is classified as memorizing, meaning that the detection strength of the method degrades with each transfer. At the same time also the image quality of the DM is much worse, as can be seen in <a href="#A2.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ Appendix B Analysis of Radioactivity for DIAGNOSIS ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">So while DIAGNOSIS displays capabilities in terms of transfer, it strongly impacts the generated image quality, making it impractical as a watermark substitution, which would make DMs <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">radioactive</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.23731v1/DIAGNOSIS_DM1.png" id="A2.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/685;" width="685" height="685" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Image generated by DM <math id="A2.F6.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> after fine-tuning on the images coated with WaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.23731v1/DIAGNOSIS_DM2.png" id="A2.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/685;" width="685" height="685" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Image generated by DM <math id="A2.F6.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> after fine-tuning on images generated by DM <math id="A2.F6.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of the outputs from DM <math id="A2.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> vs DM <math id="A2.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the DIAGNOSIS protection method.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The EDM model is trained unconditionally for 200 epochs with a mini-batch of size 512 with other hyperparameters following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. In our experiments for LDMs, we perform full finetuning of the Stable Diffusion 2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> model on the MSCOCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> or its synthetic counterpart regenerated with the LDM. We finetune all models for 10 epochs, with batch size = <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and learning rate = <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e-4</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> images.
The RAR and VAR <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> models are trained on 40000 generated watermarked ImageNet images for 10 epochs, with batch size = <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate = <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e-4</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> resolution. The other model specific hyperparameters are taken from the original training setup as specified in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. For a more consistent training we utilize only major-row scan in RAR for the whole fine-tuning process. For RAR we generate images with a <math id="A3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> of 2.0 and for VAR with a <math id="A3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> of 6.0. For both architectures we utilize a <math id="A3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.25.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional Results</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A4.T5" title="In Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we analyze the effects of passing the watermarked images through the VAE.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T5.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="A4.T5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">For <span id="A4.T5.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stable Signature</span>, <span id="A4.T5.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PRC</span>, <span id="A4.T5.13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tree-Ring</span>, and <span id="A4.T5.13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">WIAR</span> methods the watermark remains intact after being encoded into the latent space of the SD 2.1.<span id="A4.T5.13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We report the bitwise accuracy (BA) for <span id="A4.T5.13.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stable Signature</span>, and <span id="A4.T5.13.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PRC</span> (<em id="A4.T5.13.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A4.T5.13.5.4" class="ltx_text"></span> 50% corresponds to random guessing), as well as TPR@FPR=1% for <span id="A4.T5.13.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tree-Ring</span> and <span id="A4.T5.13.5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">WIAR</span> (<em id="A4.T5.13.5.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A4.T5.13.5.8" class="ltx_text"></span> 1% corresponds to random guessing).</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="A4.T5.14" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T5.14.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T5.14.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T5.14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;">Type of <math id="A4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="A4.T5.14.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T5.14.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;">Type of <math id="A4.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
