</figure>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermark transferability.</span> We perform additional experiments of the transferability of WIAR for different model sizes and cross-modality below in <a href="#A4.T8" title="In Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The results show that WIAR is radioactive in the same modality setting for different sizes (RAR to RAR and VAR to VAR). However, WIAR does not transfer cross-architecture (RAR to VAR &amp; vice versa), see last two rows below, since different encoders are used in RAR &amp; VAR, which can be patched by using a single best as in LDMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="A4.T8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">We follow the setting in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.2 Analyzing Watermark Radioactivity ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Radioactive Watermarks in Diffusion and AutoregressiveImage Generative Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and enforce transfer of a single watermarked image.</span></figcaption>
